  "main_text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is Aarav Lee from Phoenix, AZ 85025. They want to change the luggage set in their order to a coat. If that's not possible, they want to return the item or cancel the order.\n\nFirst, I found the user ID using their name and zip code, which gave me 'aarav_lee_1982'. Now, I need to check the order details. The user mentioned changing the luggage set to a coat, which is a different product type. According to the policy, modifying items in a pending order can only be done within the same product type. Since luggage and coats are different types, I can't modify the item directly.\n\nSo, the options are either return the luggage set or cancel the order. The user also said if there are any issues, cancel the entire order. I should check the order status first. If the order is pending, cancellation is possible. If it's delivered, then return would be the way to go. \n\nI need to call get_user_details to see the orders associated with the user ID. Then, check the status of each order to determine the next step. Once I have the order details, I can proceed accordingly.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"get_user_details\", \"arguments\": {\"user_id\": \"aarav_lee_1982\"}}\n",
  "continuation_text": " set in their order to a coat. If that's not possible, they want to return the item or cancel the order.\n\nFirst, I found the user ID using their name and zip code, which gave me 'aarav_lee_1982'. Now, I need to check the order details. The user mentioned changing the luggage set to a coat, which is a different product type. According to the policy, modifying items in a pending order can only be done within the same product type. Since luggage and coats are different types, I can't modify the item directly.\n\nSo, the options are either return the luggage set or cancel the order. The user also said if there are any issues, cancel the entire order. I should check the order status first. If the order is pending, cancellation is possible. If it's delivered, then return would be the way to go. \n\nI need to call get_user_details to see the orders associated with the user ID. Then, check the status of each order to determine the next step. Once I have the order details, I can proceed accordingly.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"get_user_details\", \"arguments\": {\"user_id\": \"aarav_lee_1982\"}}\n",
